Perhaps I should have phrased my question differently ...

I think that monks aren't necessarily the best choice when trying for
certain "very difficult" conduct combinations.  For example:

12-conducts: Shawn Moore did this with a gnomish healer
11-conducts: I've only ever gotten an elven priestess past infancy
here.
foodless-athiest: tourist, if you're not using "you feel like a new
man"

> I mean, lets look at intrinsics, maybe one of the major reasons to go
> for monks. How can you get them? Eating, praying and crowning are most
> likely forbidden by conducts. You can get some from eating of you are
> vegan though. The monk just gets them all and that fairly reliable.
> You start with a really nice set of them and get essentials like
> poison resistance at lvl 3.

Poison resistance is the only really vital resistance for a pre-castle
player.  All orcs and two roles (bar,hea) start with it.  Almost all
conduct combinations can pick up PR via. crowning (foodless, non-
athiest) or eating (vegan athiest).  Most (all?) the other resistances
can, with care, be acquired without breaking vegan.  The only conduct
where getting intrinsics is nearly impossible with a non-monk is
foodless athiest.

> Lets look at killing things (if you are not even pacifist). Hitting
> with a weapon is most likely out of the question, besides you can
> neither pray or wish for a good one, nor enchant it properly (if
> illiterate). Monks have no troubles with that either, just punch
> everything down and your weapon gets better, with a bit of luck you
> get gauntlets of power and you are fine. You could also kill by
> casting spells but you have to change your strategy at turn count
> 20.000 then :D (again if illiterate).

If you're going for illiterate weaponless, both samurai and monks get
kung-fu fighting.  Samurai can also get both bow and shuriken to
expert (monks are limited to basic in crossbow and shuriken).  This, I
think, outweighs the monks starting spell and better martial arts
maximum skill.

> Another point for "normal" characters is to acquire good body armor
> quickly, but you can neither wish for dragon scale mails nor can you
> produce one. As you also can't even enchant your armor very high you
> will most likely end up with a crappy AC anyways. This doesn't
> actually favor monks but makes one of their "weaknesses" void.

Not having body armour isn't that big a deal.  Combining that with a
monks typically poor starting constution does hurt though, especially
at the castle.

For example, here's what a typical character looks like when they
reach the castle:
monk: HP:75-ish, AC:-2(ish)
sam: HP:110-ish, AC:-9(ish)

With that monk, the castle can be a very dangerous place.  That
samurai, on the other hand, can do a weaponless sleepwalk to the wand
(once any liches are dealt with).

> The quest artifact is pretty awesome, as it is a guaranteed source of
